        <p
          class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300"
        >
          Cloudflare hosts the site, so every request goes through it. Like any web host, it sees your IP address, your browser, and the page you asked for, so it can send you the page and keep the site safe from attacks. I don’t keep any of that myself.
        </p>
        <p
          class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300"
        >
          I count visits with Cloudflare Web Analytics. It tells me how many people visited and which pages they looked at, but not who they were.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2
          class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold"
        >
          Your map
        </h2>
        <p
          class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300"
        >
          The countries you tick, and the day you ticked each, stay in your browser. They’re never sent to me or to anyone else, and I have no copy of them. Recommendations, visas and stamps are all worked out on your device.
        </p>
        <p
          class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300"
        >
          A share link carries your map in the part of the address after the #, which browsers don’t send to any server. It only goes where you send it, and anyone you send it to can see the countries on it. Its QR code is made on your device too.
